        "15": {
          "id": "15",
          "name": "post.html",
          "contents": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\">\n  <title>How I Actually Stay Focused While Studying | Bloom & Balance</title>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">\n</head>\n<body>\n\n  <!-- Header -->\n  <header class=\"site-header\">\n    <div class=\"container nav-container\">\n      <div class=\"site-branding\">\n        <img src=\"images/icons/logo.png\" alt=\"Bloom and Balance logo\" class=\"logo\">\n        <h1 class=\"site-title\">Bloom &amp; Balance</h1>\n      </div>\n\n      <nav class=\"main-nav\">\n        <ul>\n          <li>\n            <a href=\"index.html\">\n              <img src=\"images/icons/home-icon.png\" alt=\"Home icon\">\n              Home\n            </a>\n          </li>\n          <li>\n            <a href=\"post.html\">\n              <img src=\"images/icons/article.png\" alt=\"Articles icon\">\n              Articles\n            </a>\n          </li>\n      </nav>\n    </div>\n  </header>\n\n  <!-- Article -->\n  <main class=\"container article-page\">\n    <article class=\"full-post\">\n\n      <p class=\"post-category\">Study Tips</p>\n      <h2>How I Actually Stay Focused While Studying</h2>\n      <p class=\"post-meta\">Posted by Maya Chen · March 24, 2026</p>\n\n      <p>\n        For a long time, I thought being good at studying meant being able to sit down for hours and work without\n        getting distracted. That never really matched my real life. After school, I was usually tired, hungry,\n        and already thinking about everything else I still had to do. If I tried to force myself into a perfect\n        study session right away, I usually ended up staring at my notes without actually learning anything.\n      </p>\n\n      <p>\n        What finally helped was realizing that focus is not something that magically appears. It usually depends\n        on whether I have set myself up to work in a way that feels manageable. When I make my space calmer,\n        break my work into smaller pieces, and remove a few obvious distractions, studying feels less frustrating\n        and a lot more possible.\n      </p>\n\n      <h3>Start With a Smaller Goal</h3>\n\n      <p>\n        I used to make huge plans like “finish all my homework” or “study for every class,” which sounded productive\n        but usually made me feel overwhelmed before I even started. Now I try to begin with one specific task, like\n        reviewing vocabulary for ten minutes or outlining one paragraph for an essay. Starting smaller makes it easier\n        to begin, and once I get going, it usually feels much easier to continue.\n      </p>\n\n      <img src=\"images/article1.jpg\" alt=\"Notebook and pastel color swatches on a desk\" class=\"inline-image\">\n\n      <p>\n        This also helps me avoid wasting energy deciding what to do first. If my goal is clear, I spend less time\n        switching between tabs, rewriting my to-do list, or thinking about how much I still have left. A smaller\n        starting point gives me direction, which makes my attention feel a lot less scattered.\n      </p>\n\n      <h3>Make Your Space Work For You</h3>\n\n      <p>\n        My study space does not need to be perfect, but it does need to be usable. If my desk is covered in random\n        papers, my phone is next to me, and I have five different tabs open, I know I am going to lose focus quickly.\n        Before I begin, I try to clear enough space for the materials I actually need, put my phone somewhere out of\n        reach, and keep a drink nearby so I am not getting up every few minutes.\n      </p>\n\n      <p>\n        Even small changes make a difference. Better lighting helps me stay alert, and a cleaner desk helps me feel\n        less mentally crowded. I do not think a cute workspace solves everything, but having a calm environment does\n        make it easier to settle into the task in front of me instead of getting distracted by everything around it.\n      </p>\n\n      <img src=\"images/article2.jpg\" alt=\"Pastel study supplies and notes arranged on a desk\" class=\"inline-image\">\n\n      <h3>Take Breaks Before You Really Need Them</h3>\n\n      <p>\n        One mistake I used to make was waiting until I felt completely tired before taking a break. At that point,\n        it was much harder to come back. Now I try to pause earlier, even if it is just for a few minutes to stretch,\n        refill my water, or walk around the room. A short break helps me reset without fully losing momentum.\n      </p>\n\n      <p>\n        I have learned that staying focused is less about having perfect discipline and more about noticing what makes\n        it easier to keep going. The best routine is usually not the most intense one. It is the one that feels simple\n        enough to repeat, even on days when motivation is low and there are a lot of other things competing for your\n        attention.\n      </p>\n\n    </article>\n  </main>\n\n  <!-- Footer -->\n  <footer class=\"site-footer\">\n    <div class=\"container\">\n      <p>&copy; 2026 Bloom &amp; Balance Blog. All rights reserved.</p>\n    </div>\n  </footer>\n\n</body>\n</html>",
